#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:0
msgid ""
"Learn how to list, find, install, run, update and remove snap packages with "
"a hands-on introduction to using snap packages for new users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:9
msgid "Get started"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:11
msgid ""
"A snap is a bundle of one or more applications that works without "
"dependencies or modification across many different Linux distributions. "
"Snaps are discoverable and installable from the [Snap Store](https://"
"snapcraft.io/store), a public app store with an audience of millions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:13
msgid ""
"This tour introduces all of snap's main features. We suggest going through "
"the first few steps and then playing with what you've learnt. Come back when "
"you feel comfortable and wish to further your knowledge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:15
msgid ""
"By the end of the tour, you'll have a good understanding of how to use "
"snaps, from how they're installed and updated, to how they're backed-up and "
"removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:17
msgid "Requirements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:19
msgid ""
"Snaps can be installed and removed with a graphical package manager, such as "
"Ubuntu Software Centre, but most advanced functionality is only available "
"from the Linux command shell."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:21
msgid ""
"The command shell is accessible from [Terminal](https://ubuntu.com/tutorials/"
"command-line-for-beginners#1-overview) and many similar applications. It "
"helps if you have some familiarity with this, but if you don't, this tour is "
"itself an ideal introduction to get you started."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:23
msgid ""
"Many Linux distributions, including Ubuntu, support _snap_ by default. You "
"can check by running the `snap` command. If the snap command is not found, "
"take a look at our {ref}`Installation guides <tutorials-install-the-daemon-"
"index>` for further help."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:25
msgid "List installed snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:27
msgid ""
"Snap is installed with a few other snaps, and a good place to start is to "
"display these with the `snap list` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:29
msgid ""
"$ snap list\n"
"Name             Version        Rev    Tracking         Publisher   Notes\n"
"core22           20231123       1033   latest/stable    canonical✓  base\n"
"firefox          120.0.1-1      3504   latest/stable    mozilla✓    -\n"
"snapd            2.60.4         20290  latest/stable    canonical✓  snapd\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:37
msgid ""
"Versions and revisions, under the _Version_ and _Rev_ columns respectively, "
"convey different details about one specific release of a snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:39
msgid ""
"_Version_ : the version of the software being packaged, as assigned by the "
"developers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:40
msgid ""
"_Revision_: the sequence number assigned by the store when the snap file was "
"uploaded"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:42
msgid ""
"The *version* is a name or number that was arbitrarily assigned to a release "
"by its developers, according to their development practices. It tells the "
"user what content to expect from a snap. The *revision* is an automatic "
"number assigned by the store to give every snap release a unique identity "
"within the channel across every architecture supported by the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:44
msgid "Finding snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:46
msgid ""
"There are snaps for many popular applications, including [Spotify](https://"
"snapcraft.io/spotify), [Slack](https://snapcraft.io/slack) and the [Chromium "
"web browser](https://snapcraft.io/chromium)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:48
msgid ""
"The best way to find new snaps is to use the online [Snap Store](https://"
"snapcraft.io/store), either by searching for apps and words you’re "
"interested in, such as \"Spotify\", “music” or “maths”, or by browsing "
"through categories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:50
msgid ""
"To search for snaps with ‘media player’ in either their names or "
"descriptions, type `snap find \"media player\"` into your terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:52
msgid ""
"$ snap find \"media player\"\n"
"Name  Version  Developer    Notes  Summary\n"
"vlc   3.0.4     videolan✓   -      The ultimate media player.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:58
msgid ""
"The `✓` alongside *videolan* in the above output indicates that the snap "
"publisher has been _verified_. Verified publishers are trusted to produce "
"and maintain high-quality packages and include institutions, foundations, "
"and companies."
msgstr ""

