#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:72
msgid "Learn about a snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:74
msgid ""
"The `snap info` command makes it easy to find more details about a specific "
"snap. These details include what a snap does, who publishes it, which "
"commands it provides."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:76
msgid "The final part of the output lists the _channels_ for the snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:78
msgid ""
"channels:\n"
"  latest/stable:    3.0.19                      2023-10-13 (3721) 336MB -\n"
"  latest/candidate: 3.0.19                      2023-10-02 (3721) 336MB -\n"
"  latest/beta:      3.0.20-27-g795b1bc62b       2023-12-13 (3862) 336MB -\n"
"  latest/edge:      4.0.0-dev-26928-g9bc7ded0f0 2023-12-13 (3863) 692MB -\n"
"installed:          3.0.19                                 (3721) 336MB -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:87
msgid ""
"{ref}`Channels <explanation-how-snaps-work-channels-and-tracks>` declare "
"which release of a snap is installed and tracked for updates. The *latest/"
"stable* channel is used by default, but opting to install from a different "
"channel can be useful for testing new features, or for installing old legacy "
"versions of an application. Which snaps are released to which channels is "
"entirely up to the snap publisher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:89
msgid "Install the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:91
msgid ""
"Type `snap install` followed by the name of the snap to install the snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:93
msgid "sudo snap install vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:97
msgid ""
"When _install_ is run for the first time, one or more dependencies may be "
"automatically installed alongside the snap you requested. Your network speed "
"will determine how long this process takes. Snap operations can always be "
"safely cancelled with ctrl+c, and one of snap's best features is that an "
"operation is either wholly successful, or it's cleanly rolled back to the "
"previous state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:99
msgid "A channel can also be optionally specified with the `channel` option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:101
msgid "sudo snap install --channel=edge vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:105
msgid ""
"After installation, the channel being monitored for updates can be changed "
"with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:107
msgid "sudo snap switch --channel=stable vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:111
msgid "Run apps and commands from the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:113
msgid ""
"The vast majority of snap-installed applications will run as you expect, "
"from either the command line or from the desktop launcher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:115
msgid ""
"If executing a command directly doesn't work, use the `snap run` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:117
msgid "snap run vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:121
msgid ""
"Links to snapped applications are located in `/snap/bin` which is added to "
"the system \\$PATH."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:123
msgid "Update an installed snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:125
msgid ""
"Snaps are updated automatically. However, to manually check for updates, use "
"the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:127
msgid "sudo snap refresh vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:131
msgid ""
"The above will check the channel being tracked by the snap. If a newer "
"version of the snap is available, it will be downloaded and installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:133
msgid ""
"Changing the channel being tracked and refreshing the snap can be "
"accomplished with a single command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:135
msgid "sudo snap refresh --channel=beta vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:139
msgid ""
"Updates are automatically installed within 6 hours of a revision being made "
"to a tracked channel, keeping most systems up-to-date. This schedule can be "
"tuned via configuration options and disabled with the `--hold` option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:141
msgid "To check which channel a snap is tracking use the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:143
msgid "snap refresh --tracking vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:147
msgid "Pause or stop automatic updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:149
msgid ""
"The `snap refresh --hold` command holds, or postpones, snap updates for "
"individual snaps, or for all snaps on the system, either indefinitely or for "
"a specified period of time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:151
msgid "snap refresh --hold=<duration> <snap1> <snap2>...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:155
msgid ""
"Time duration units can be seconds (s), minutes (m) or hours (h), or a "
"combination of these.  To postpone updates indefinitely, a value of "
"`forever` is also valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:157
msgid ""
"$ snap refresh --hold=24h firefox\n"
"General refreshes of \"firefox\" held until 2023-10-26T14:10:53+01:00\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:162
msgid "If no duration is specified, the hold period defaults to `forever`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:164
msgid ""
"Refer to {ref}`Managing updates <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-manage-"
"updates>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:166
msgid "Revert to an earlier revision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:168
msgid ""
"A snap may be reverted to an earlier revision with the `snap revert` "
"command. By default, it will attempt to revert to the previous revision:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:170
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap revert vlc\n"
"vlc reverted to 3.0.5-1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:175
msgid ""
"The optional `--revision` argument can be specified to request a specific "
"revision:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:177
msgid "snap revert vlc --revision 500\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:181
msgid ""
"This operation will revert both the snap revision and the configuration data "
"associated with the software. If the previously used revision of the snap is "
"from a different channel, that snap will be installed but the channel being "
"tracked won't change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:183
msgid ""
"User data, such as data generated by the snap and stored in a database, is "
"often stored in a _common_ directory and will not be reverted. See {ref}"
"`Data locations <interfaces-data-locations>` for more details on what "
"information is stored and where."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:185
msgid ""
"A snap won't automatically update to a version previously reverted from, and "
"the output from `snap refresh` will continue to state *All snaps up to "
"date*. A reverted snap will be automatically updated when a new and "
"different revision is made available by the publisher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:187
msgid ""
"However, explicitly adding the snap name to `snap refresh` *will* update the "
"snap, regardless of whether the latest revision was previously reverted from "
"or not:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:189
msgid ""
"$ snap list --all vlc\n"
"Name  Version  Rev  Tracking  Publisher  Notes\n"
"vlc   3.0.5-1  768  stable    videolan✓  -\n"
"vlc   3.0.6    770  stable    videolan✓  disabled\n"
"$ sudo snap refresh\n"
"All snaps up to date.\n"
"$ sudo snap refresh vlc\n"
"vlc 3.0.6 from VideoLAN✓ refreshed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:200
msgid ""
"A previously used snap that was reverted from will display *disabled* in the "
"Notes column of the output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:202
msgid "Connect an interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:204
msgid ""
"Interfaces put you in control of what applications can and cannot do with "
"your system by either permitting or denying access to resources outside a "
"snap's confinement. They're most commonly used to enable access to a webcam, "
"to permit sound recording, for network access, or to read and write to your "
"$HOME directory or remote storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:206
msgid ""
"Which interfaces a snap requires, and *provides*, depends on the type of "
"snap and its own requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:208
msgid ""
"To see which interfaces a snap is using, and which interfaces it could use "
"but isn't, type `snap connections <snapname>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:210
msgid ""
"$ snap connections vlc\n"
"Interface       Plug                   Slot                 Notes\n"
"audio-playback  vlc:audio-playback     :audio-playback      -\n"
"audio-record    vlc:audio-record       -                    -\n"
"camera          vlc:camera             -                    -\n"
"desktop         vlc:desktop            :desktop             -\n"
"home            vlc:home               :home                -\n"
"(...)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:221
msgid ""
"The slot is the provider of the resource while the plug is the consumer, and "
"a slot can support multiple plug connections. In the above output, the {ref}"
"`camera <interfaces-camera-interface>` interface is not connected because "
"its slot is empty. This means VLC cannot access any connected cameras. The "
"`<snap-name>:<interface-name>` syntax describes which snap is responsible "
"for which component. If there's no snap, such as with `:audio-playback`, the "
"component is directly connected to the system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:223
msgid ""
"To allow a camera to be accessible to VLC, the interface can be connected "
"with the `snap connect` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:225
msgid "snap connect vlc:camera\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:228
msgid ""
"<!--\n"
"\n"
"The Ubuntu Software/[Snap Store](https://snapcraft.io/snap-store) desktop "
"application can also be used to enable and disable interface connections.\n"
"\n"
"To access its interface management functions, either search for an installed "
"snap, or select it from the *Manage* view. The interfaces for the selected "
"application can then be viewed by selecting **Permissions** :\n"
"\n"
"![Snap Store VLC interface connections](https://assets.ubuntu.com/v1/"
"a2609611-store-05.png)\n"
"\n"
"-->\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:238
msgid ""
"As you can see the output from `snap connections vlc`, and in the above "
"image, VLC  already has access a  user's _/home_ directory because the {ref}"
"`home <interfaces-home-interface>` interface is connected to the system "
"`$HOME` directory. This is an {ref}`automatic connection <explanation-"
"interfaces-interface-auto-connection>`, and is granted to certain interfaces "
"and snaps when an interface provides fundamental functionality, such as VLC "
"accessing your personal video and audio files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:240
msgid ""
"Refer to {ref}`Interfaces <explanation-interfaces-all-about-interfaces>` for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:242
msgid "Where snaps store data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:244
msgid ""
"Most snaps use strict confinement. This isolates both their execution "
"environments and their data from your system (see {ref}`Snap Confinement "
"<explanation-security-snap-confinement>` for further details). A confined "
"snap that needs user-access to files will most likely use the {ref}`home "
"<interfaces-home-interface>` interface to bridge this confinement gap, "
"allowing you to save and load files from your home directory automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:246
msgid ""
"You can see whether the _home_ interface is being used in the output to "
"`snap connections <snap name>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:248
msgid ""
"$ snap connections nethack\n"
"Interface  Plug          Slot   Notes\n"
"home       nethack:home  :home  -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:254
msgid ""
"Regardless of whether the home interface is used or not, a snap can also "
"store user data, such as a database or configuration files, within its own "
"directory under _$HOME/snap_. Data within this snap-specific directory is "
"stored in one of two further directories, depending on whether the data "
"needs to be tied to a specific release, or whether it can be used across "
"multiple releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:256
msgid ""
"Data for a specific release is stored within a directory named after the "
"{ref}`revision <reference-operations-glossary>` of a release. This is a "
"numeric value, such as `55` or `56`. The data for each specific revision is "
"often copied from one release to the next, so that reverting from one "
"revision to a previous revision will restore a working configuration, for "
"instance. The snap directory also contains a symbolic link called `current` "
"that points to the snap revision currently active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:258
msgid ""
"Data that can be shared across releases is stored in a directory called "
"`common`, and might include image or audio caches, or a database. This data "
"is not copied between releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:260
msgid ""
"For more details on where snaps store their data, see {ref}`Data locations "
"<interfaces-data-locations>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:262
msgid "Create and restore a snapshot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:264
msgid ""
"A *snapshot* is a copy of the user, system and configuration data stored by "
"*snapd* for one or more snaps on your system, and a snapshot of the data "
"found in both `$HOME/snap/<snap-name>` and `/var/snap/<snap-name>` is stored "
"in `/var/lib/snapd/snapshots/` (see {ref}`Data locations <interfaces-data-"
"locations>` for more details)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:266
msgid ""
"Snapshots are generated **manually** with the `snap save` command and "
"**automatically when a snap is removed**. A snapshot can be used to backup "
"the state of your snaps, revert snaps to a previous state and to restore a "
"fresh snapd installation to a previously saved state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:268
msgid ""
"The `snap save` command creates a snapshot for all installed snaps, or if "
"declared individually, specific snaps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:270
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap save\n"
"Set  Snap         Age    Version               Rev   Size   Notes\n"
"30   core         1.00s  16-2.37~pre1          6229   250B  -\n"
"30   core18       886ms  18                    543    123B  -\n"
"30   go           483ms  1.10.7                3092   387B  -\n"
"30   vlc          529ms  3.0.6                 770   882kB  -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:279
msgid ""
"The `restore` command replaces the current user, system and configuration "
"data with the corresponding data from the specified snapshot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:281
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap restore 30\n"
"Restored snapshot #30.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:286
msgid ""
"By default, this command restores all the data for all the snaps in a "
"snapshot. You can restore data for specific snaps by simply listing them "
"after the command, and for specific users with the `--users=<usernames>` "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:288
msgid ""
"Excluding a snap’s system and configuration data from *snap restore* is not "
"currently possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:290
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snapshots <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` for "
"further details on creating, exporting, importing and validating snapshots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:292
msgid "Remove a snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:294
msgid ""
"To remove a snap from your system, along with its internal user, system and "
"configuration data, use the *remove* command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:296
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap remove vlc\n"
"vlc removed\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:301
msgid ""
"Add the `--no-wait` option to return immediately to the command prompt and "
"run the removal in the background."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:303
msgid ""
"By default, all of a snap's revisions are also removed. To remove a specific "
"revision, add the `--revision=<revision-number>` argument to the *remove* "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:305
msgid ""
"Prior to removal (except on [Ubuntu Core](https://www.ubuntu.com/core) "
"systems), a snap's internal user, system, and configuration data is saved as "
"a *snapshot* (_snapd 2.39+_), and retained for 31 days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:307
msgid ""
"To  remove a snap without generating a snapshot, use the additional `--"
"purge` argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/get-started.md:309
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap remove vlc --purge\n"
"vlc removed\n"
msgstr ""

